<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Joan of Arc</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 1412–<a href="/years/1431">1431</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/15th-century">15th century</a> · Also known as Jeanne d'Arc, Maid of Orléans</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Joan of Arc was a peasant girl from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/domremy-la-pucelle">Domrémy</a>, on the borders of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lorraine">Lorraine</a> and Champagne, who believed that saints' voices called her to save <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hundred-years-war">Hundred Years' War</a>. In 1429 she helped lift the English <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/siege-of-orleans">siege of Orléans</a> and brought Charles VII to his coronation at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/reims">Reims</a>. Captured by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/burgundians">Burgundians</a>, she was condemned for heresy by a pro-English church court and burned at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rouen">Rouen</a> in 1431; she was canonised in 1920.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/joan-of-arc.jpg" alt="Joan of Arc" width="320" height="477" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Joan_of_Arc_miniature_graded.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote><b>Joan of Arc</b> came from Domrémy on the borders of Lorraine and Champagne and visited Duke Charles II at Nancy in 1429.</blockquote><a href="/empire/duchy-of-lorraine">Duchy of Lorraine · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>Recovery came with <b>Joan of Arc</b>'s relief of Orléans in 1429 and with Charles VII's reforms, which in 1445 created the compagnies d'ordonnance, the first permanent royal cavalry, and used artillery to win at Formigny in 1450 and Castillon in 1453.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-france">Kingdom of France · Military</a></li><li><blockquote><b>Joan of Arc</b> entered Orléans with supplies and reinforcements.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/april-29">29 April in history</a></li><li><blockquote>By tradition <b>Joan of Arc</b> was born on Epiphany in the village of Domrémy, in north-eastern France.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/january-6">6 January in history</a></li><li><blockquote>A church court led by Bishop Pierre Cauchon, an ally of the English, began proceedings against <b>Joan of Arc</b> on charges of heresy.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/january-9">9 January in history</a></li><li><blockquote>Charles VII, king of France since 1422, was crowned in Reims Cathedral, the traditional place of French coronations, after <b>Joan of Arc</b> had raised the siege of Orléans.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/july-17">17 July in history</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/domremy-la-pucelle">Domrémy-la-Pucelle</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/france">France</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rouen">Rouen</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/france">France</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=48.44,5.68&amp;year=1424" aria-label="Domrémy-la-Pucelle on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4682 470 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Joan of Arc"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5132 740h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5132 740h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=48.44,5.68&amp;year=1424">What was here in 1424? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
<section class="enc-words" id="in-their-words"><h2 class="enc-section-title">In their words</h2><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;If I am not, may God place me there; if I am, may God so keep me. I should be the saddest in all the world if I knew that I were not in the grace of God.&#8221;</p><footer>Trial of Condemnation, 1431 &#183; tr. T. Douglas Murray, 1902 &#183; her answer when asked whether she knew herself to be in the grace of God &#183; <a href="https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/57389/pg57389-images.html" rel="noopener">Project Gutenberg</a></footer></blockquote><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;I was thirteen when I had a Voice from God for my help and guidance. The first time that I heard this Voice, I was very much frightened; it was mid-day, in the summer, in my father’s garden.&#8221;</p><footer>Trial of Condemnation, 1431 &#183; tr. T. Douglas Murray, 1902 &#183; her answer to the judges &#183; <a href="https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/57389/pg57389-images.html" rel="noopener">Project Gutenberg</a></footer></blockquote><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;Yes, I learnt to spin and to sew; in sewing and spinning I fear no woman in Rouen.&#8221;</p><footer>Trial of Condemnation, 1431 &#183; tr. T. Douglas Murray, 1902 &#183; asked whether she had learnt a trade in her youth &#183; <a href="https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/57389/pg57389-images.html" rel="noopener">Project Gutenberg</a></footer></blockquote><details class="enc-q-more"><summary>3 more</summary><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;I am come in God’s name; I have nothing to do here; let me be sent back to God, whence I came.&#8221;</p><footer>Trial of Condemnation, 1431 &#183; tr. T. Douglas Murray, 1902 &#183; <a href="https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/57389/pg57389-images.html" rel="noopener">Project Gutenberg</a></footer></blockquote><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;It was I, myself, who bore this banner, when I attacked the enemy, to save killing any one, for I have never killed any one.&#8221;</p><footer>Trial of Condemnation, 1431 &#183; tr. T. Douglas Murray, 1902 &#183; <a href="https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/57389/pg57389-images.html" rel="noopener">Project Gutenberg</a></footer></blockquote><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;Better, forty times better, my banner than my sword!&#8221;</p><footer>Trial of Condemnation, 1431, asked whether she loved her banner or her sword more &#183; tr. T. Douglas Murray, 1902 &#183; <a href="https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/57389/pg57389-images.html" rel="noopener">Project Gutenberg</a></footer></blockquote></details><p class="enc-article-foot"><a class="enc-random-link" href="/encyclopedia/quotations">Quotations from 535 people &rarr;</a></p></section>
